/*
# DataGrid

The Table contract plus a **column model**: headers, sortable, reorderable and
resizable by drag, touch-correct, over a windowed collection.

    DataGrid [ width = 600, height = 400, datapath = { app.d.value },
        selects = "multi",
        Column [ title = "ID",    field = "id",    width = 70 ],
        Column [ title = "Title", field = "title", width = 280 ],
        Column [ title = "State", field = "state", width = 110 ],
        GridRow [ datapath = :rows[], virtualize = true ] ]

- **Columns are written members** (`Column`: permanent, authored, its identity
  the child itself): `title` heads the column, `field` names what a cell
  shows, `width` is the authored width — a plain value (the column model
  follows which columns exist, not a column's own attributes; a responsive
  width belongs in the grid's `widths` state, which is a tracked dependency:
  `widths = { app.narrow ? ({ id: 40, … }) : null }`). A column's id is its
  `field` (title as fallback).
- **Order and widths are the grid's value-pattern state**: `order` (column
  ids, presented order) and `widths` (id → width overrides). Reorder delivers
  through `arrangeInput(order)`, resize through `resizeInput(id, w)` — the
  defaults write the attributes; an app override owns them (persisted layouts).
  Widths are plain values — nothing measures cells.
- **Header interactions**: click sorts — a delivery (`sortInput(on, dir)`,
  writing `sortOn`/`sortDir` by default): sort names a derivation, the truth
  stays in data (the app derives the sorted dataset; a grid displays the
  order its collection has). Horizontal drag reorders (the new order commits
  on release); the right-edge grip resizes. Both drags declare `claim = x` —
  on touch the page keeps vertical pan while the drag owns x.
- **Rows**: the use site writes one bare `GridRow` template over its rows
  path — cells generate from the column model, each showing `record[field]`.
  Selection, keyboard, windowing: Table's, unchanged — `virtualize` on the
  template windows the grid, and the header (a preceding sibling) offsets
  the window's geometry.
- Focus: the grid lands as one tab stop, in browse mode — arrows navigate
  rows.
- Narrow widths: a cell clips; a `widths` entry of `0` drops its column
  entirely — priority, not squish.

Selection, keyboard travel and windowing are `Table`'s, documented there. What follows
is only the column model.

```declare
App [ width = 440, height = 220,
    d: Dataset { { "rows": [ { "id": 1, "title": "Fix the rail",      "state": "open" },
                             { "id": 2, "title": "Draft the chapter", "state": "done" },
                             { "id": 3, "title": "Measure the grid",  "state": "open" } ] } },
    DataGrid [ x = 20, y = 50, width = 400, height = 130, datapath = { d.value },
        selects = "multi",
        Column [ title = "ID",    field = "id",    width = 50 ],
        Column [ title = "Title", field = "title", width = 220 ],
        Column [ title = "State", field = "state", width = 110 ],
        GridRow [ datapath = :rows[] ]
        ]
    ]
```

Drag a header sideways to reorder; drag the divider between two headers to resize;
click a header to sort.

## order
The presented column order, as column ids — `null` means authored order. Part of the
grid's value-pattern state: delivered through `arrangeInput`, so an app that overrides
that owns the order and can persist it.

## widths
Per-column width overrides, `{ id: width }` — `null` means authored widths. **A width of
`0` drops the column entirely**, which is the deliberate narrow-screen degrade: priority,
not squish. `widths = { app.narrow ? ({ notes: 0 }) : null }` is a responsive layout.

## sortOn
The column id the sort *names* — `""` when unsorted. **Sorting names a derivation; it
does not reorder anything.** The truth stays in the data: your app derives the sorted
dataset from this, and the grid displays the order its collection already has.

## sortDir
`"asc"` or `"desc"`, alongside `sortOn`.

## sortInput()
**Delivery seam for sorting.** The default writes `sortOn`/`sortDir`; override it to own
the sort — which is what you do when the sorted collection is derived app-side, or when
the sort belongs to a server query.

## arrangeInput()
**Delivery seam for reordering**, receiving the new column-id order. Override it to
persist a user's column layout.

## resizeInput()
**Delivery seam for resizing**, receiving a column id and its new width. Widths are
plain values — **nothing measures cells** — so an override is free to clamp, snap, or
store them.

## headerClick()
Toggles the sort on a column: same column flips direction, a new column starts
ascending. Routes through `sortInput`.

## headerH
The header row's height.

## editStep()
Moves an active `"edit"` cell's focus one row up or down in the same column — the
spreadsheet expectation. Scrolls the destination into existence first, so it works over
a windowed collection.

## openSelect()
Opens the shared options menu for a `"select"` cell and writes the picked value into the
record through the row's cursor. **One menu serves every select cell in the grid.**

## columnRecords()
The derived column model — the authored `Column` children, ordered and widthed by state,
with running x offsets. **The one truth every header cell and grid cell positions from.**
Its inputs arrive as arguments — the grid's `childViews`, `order` and `widths` — so the
model re-derives when a column is added or removed, or the order or widths change.

## currentOrder()
The column ids in their present order.

## colId()
A column's id: its `field`, falling back to its `title`.

## colWidthOf()
The present width of a column by id.

## _dragCol
**Internal.** The column id currently being dragged.

## _dropAt
**Internal.** The index the insertion bar marks, or −1.

## _dropIndexFor()
**Internal.** Which slot a dragged header's centre would land in.

## _dropBarX()
**Internal.** The insertion bar's x for a drop index.

## _commitDrop()
**Internal.** Commits a reorder at release — drop-commit, since swapping under the
pointer re-derives the spans mid-drag and flickers.

## _prevColId()
**Internal.** The column left of a given one, for the resize grip.

## _selMenu
**Internal.** The lazily-created menu shared by every `"select"` cell.

## _isDataGrid
**Internal.** The marker rows and headers guard on — not API.

## inHeader
Whether the keyboard position is in the header row: Up from the first row goes there,
Left and Right move along the headers, Enter or Space sorts by the column, Down comes back.

## activeCol
The active cell's column — with Table's active row, the grid's keyboard position. Left and Right move it.

## activeCell()
The active cell's view, when its row is built.

## enterCell()
Enters a cell: focuses an edit cell's field (starting it with a typed character, when one is given), focuses a check cell's box, or opens a select cell's menu.

## onKeyDown
Left and Right move the active cell; Enter, F2 or a typed character edits it; Space toggles a check cell; the rest is Table's.
*/

include [ "table.declare", "menu.declare" ]

use [ Menu ]

// A column declaration — a data-only member (invisible; SimpleLayout skips
// it and reclaims its space). `width` is the View attribute, reused as the
// authored column width. `kind` declares what a cell is — each kind its own
// cell class, below:
//   "text"   — TextCell, a static label (the default)
//   "edit"   — EditCell, a TextInput committing each edit into the record (live)
//   "check"  — CheckCell, a Checkbox over a boolean field
//   "select" — SelectCell, the value + a chevron; a tap opens the shared options menu
// `options` feeds "select" (records or strings, shown by String()).

/*
# Column

One column of a `DataGrid`: a written member, never replicated — permanent, authored,
its identity the child itself. `title` heads it, `field` names what each cell shows,
`kind` says what a cell is, and `width` is its authored width. A column's id is its
`field`, with `title` as the fallback. A column draws nothing itself (`visible` is
false): the grid builds headers and cells from the column model.

```declare
App [ width = 400, height = 170,
    d: Dataset { { "rows": [ { "id": 1, "task": "Write", "done": true }, { "id": 2, "task": "Ship", "done": false } ] } },
    DataGrid [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 340, height = 110, datapath = { d.value },
        Column [ title = "Task", field = "task", kind = "edit", width = 220 ],
        Column [ title = "Done", field = "done", kind = "check", width = 80 ],
        GridRow [ datapath = :rows[] ]
        ]
    ]
```

## title
The column's heading text. Also its identity when `field` is empty.

## field
The record key this column shows, and the column's id. **A cell renders
`record[field]`** — there is no per-cell template to write.

## kind
What a cell *is*: `"text"` (the default, a static label), `"edit"` (a `TextInput`
committing every keystroke into the record), `"check"` (a `Checkbox` over a boolean
field), or `"select"` (the value plus a chevron, opening the shared options menu). Each
kind is its own cell class — `TextCell`, `EditCell`, `CheckCell`, `SelectCell` — and a
column's cells are built as that class alone.

## options
The choices a `"select"` cell offers — records or strings. **Read at gesture time, not
baked into the column model**, so it may be a constraint that changes with app state.

## _isColumn
**Internal.** The marker the column walk keys on — not API.

*/

class Column [
    _isColumn: boolean = true,

    visible = false,
    title: string = "",
    field: string = "",
    kind: string = "text",
    options: array = null,
    width = 120
    ]


class DataGrid extends Table [
    accessibilityRole = grid,

    // the marker rows and headers guard on

    _isDataGrid: boolean = true,

    headerH: number = { provided("theme").gridHeaderHeight },

    // ── the column-model state — the value pattern ────────────────────────

    order: array = null,        // column ids, presented order (null = authored)
    widths: object = null,      // { id: width } overrides (null = authored)
    sortOn: string = "",        // the column id the sort names ("" = unsorted)
    sortDir: string = "",       // "asc" | "desc"

    // ── the delivery seams — defaults write the attributes; use-site overrides
    //    own the state (persisted layouts, app-derived sorts) ──────────────

    sortInput(on: string, dir: string) { sortOn = on; sortDir = dir },
    arrangeInput(ord: array) { order = ord },
    resizeInput(id: string, w: number) { widths = ({ ...(widths ?? ({})), [id]: w }) },

    headerClick(id: string) {
        this.sortInput(id, sortOn == id && sortDir == "asc" ? "desc" : "asc")
        },

    // ── the column records: the authored Columns, ordered and widthed by
    //    the state, with running x offsets — the one derived truth every
    //    header cell and grid cell positions from. A for-of walk over the
    //    grid's childViews (never .filter in constraint-reachable code); the
    //    children and the state ride in as arguments so the deriving
    //    constraint tracks them ─────────────────────────────────────────────

    columnRecords(kids: View[], ord: array, w: object) -> array {
        const cols = []
        for (const c of kids) { if ((c as Column)._isColumn === true) cols.push(c) }
        if (ord != null) {
            const rank = (c) => {
                const i = ord.indexOf(this.colId(c))
                return i < 0 ? ord.length + cols.indexOf(c) : i
                }
            cols.sort((a, b) => rank(a) - rank(b))
            }
        const out = []
        let x = 0
        for (const c of cols) {
            const id = this.colId(c)
            const wd = w != null && typeof (w as any)[id] == "number" ? (w as any)[id] : c.width
            // width 0 drops the column — the deliberate narrow degrade
            // (priority, not squish): a responsive `widths` state zeroes the
            // low-priority columns and the model omits them entirely
            if (wd <= 0) continue
            out.push({ id: id, title: (c as Column).title, field: (c as Column).field, width: wd, x: x,
                kind: (c as Column).kind, i: out.length })
            x = x + wd
            }
        return out
        },

    colId(c: object) -> string {
        return "" + ((c as Column).field != "" ? (c as Column).field : (c as Column).title)
        },

    currentOrder() -> array { return this.colData.value.cols.map((r) => r.id) },

    colData: Dataset [ contents = { { cols: classroot.columnRecords(classroot.childViews, classroot.order, classroot.widths) } } ],

    // reorder-by-drag commits on release, never live: swapping under the
    // pointer would re-derive the spans mid-drag and flicker. While a header
    // drags, `_dragCol`/`_dropAt` drive the insertion bar (the vertical divider
    // at the would-be slot), and the order commits at the drop.

    _dragCol: string = "",
    _dropAt: number = -1,

    _dropIndexFor(cx: number) -> number {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        for (let i = 0; i < cols.length; i = i + 1) {
            if (cx < cols[i].x + cols[i].width / 2) return i
            }
        return cols.length
        },

    // `at` rides in as an argument so the insertion bar's constraint tracks
    // it, as `columnRecords` takes its state

    _dropBarX(at: number) -> number {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        if (at < 0) return -10
        if (at >= cols.length) {
            const last = cols[cols.length - 1]
            return last != null ? last.x + last.width : 0
            }
        return cols[at].x
        },

    _commitDrop(id: string) {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        const from = cols.findIndex((r) => r.id == id)
        const at = _dropAt
        _dragCol = ""
        _dropAt = -1
        if (from < 0 || at < 0) return
        const to = at > from ? at - 1 : at
        if (to == from) return
        const ord = cols.map((r) => r.id)
        ord.splice(from, 1)
        ord.splice(to, 0, id)
        this.arrangeInput(ord)
        },

    // ── the active cell: the keyboard position in two dimensions — the active
    //    row is Table's, the column is this. Left/Right move the column; Enter,
    //    F2 or a typed character enters the cell; inside it, Tab walks the row's
    //    cells and Esc comes back (planes.md §13.6.2) ──────────────────────

    activeCol: number = 0,

    // the keyboard position may be in the header row: Up from the first row goes
    // there, Left and Right move along the headers, Enter or Space sorts by the
    // column, Down comes back to the rows

    inHeader: boolean = false,

    activeCell() -> GridCell? {
        const inst = this.children.find((c) => (c as TableRow)._isTableRow === true && (c as TableRow).tableIndex() == activeIndex)
        if (inst == null) return null
        return ((inst as GridRow).cells.children.find((c) => (c as GridCell)._ci == activeCol) ?? null) as GridCell | null
        },

    enterCell(cell: GridCell, typed: string) {
        if (cell._k == "edit") {
            const ed = (cell as EditCell).ed
            if (typed == "") { Focus.focus(ed); return }
            // a typed character starts the edit with it: after this keypress,
            // so the key is not typed a second time into the field it moved to
            afterDelay(0, () => {
                Focus.focus(ed)                     // first: the edit records the value it began from
                if (cell.row != null) cell.row.setText(cell._fld, typed)
                afterSettle(() => { ed.select("end") })
                })
            }
        else if (cell._k == "check") Focus.focus((cell as CheckCell).ck)
        else if (cell._k == "select") (cell as SelectCell).sel.press()
        },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        const n = this.colData.value.cols.length
        const plain = !e.meta && !e.ctrl && !e.alt
        if (e.key == "ArrowRight" && plain) { activeCol = Math.min(n - 1, activeCol + 1); return }
        if (e.key == "ArrowLeft" && plain) { activeCol = Math.max(0, activeCol - 1); return }
        if (inHeader) {
            if (e.key == "ArrowDown" && plain) inHeader = false
            else if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == " ") && plain && !e.repeat) {
                const c = this.colData.value.cols[activeCol]
                if (c != null) this.headerClick("" + c.id)
                }
            return
            }
        if (e.key == "ArrowUp" && plain && activeIndex <= 0) { inHeader = true; return }
        const cell = this.activeCell()
        if (cell != null && plain && !e.repeat) {
            if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == "F2") && cell._k != "text") { this.enterCell(cell, ""); return }
            if (e.key == " " && cell._k == "check") {
                if (cell.row != null) cell.row.setFlag(cell._fld, cell.value != true)
                return
                }
            if (e.key == " " && cell._k == "select") { this.enterCell(cell, ""); return }
            if (cell._k == "edit" && e.key.length == 1 && e.key != " ") { this.enterCell(cell, e.key); return }
            }
        super.onKeyDown(e)
        },

    // vertical arrows from an active editor cell: the edit moves to the
    // same column one row up/down (the spreadsheet expectation) — the
    // destination scrolls into existence and its editor takes focus.
    //
    // The focus waits for the next frame (afterDelay 0) rather than an afterSettle
    // step because showRow scrolls the platform scroller, and that scroll
    // lands as an event after this settle: the windowed rows re-link on it,
    // and an editor whose row element moves loses the focus it was just
    // given. By the next frame the rows have re-seated.

    editStep(row: View, field: string, d: number) {
        const i = (row as TableRow).tableIndex() + d
        if (i < 0 || i >= this.itemCount()) return
        this.showRow(i)
        afterDelay(0, () => {
            const inst = this.children.find((c) => (c as TableRow)._isTableRow === true && (c as TableRow).tableIndex() == i)
            if (inst == null) return
            const cell = (inst as GridRow).cells.children.find((c) => "" + (c as any)._fld == field) as any
            if (cell != null && cell.ed != null) Focus.focus(cell.ed)
            })
        },

    _prevColId(id: string) -> string {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        const i = cols.findIndex((r) => r.id == id)
        return i > 0 ? cols[i - 1].id : ""
        },

    colWidthOf(id: string) -> number {
        const cols = this.colData.value.cols
        const r = cols.find((c) => c.id == id)
        return r != null ? r.width : 0
        },

    // ── the select-cell options menu: one app-layer Menu shared by every
    //    select cell in the grid (as Combobox hosts its list — created by
    //    name at first use, retargeted per cell); picking writes the record
    //    through the row's cursor, and the cell re-derives like any other
    //    read ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

    _selMenu: View? = null,

    openSelect(row: View, cell: View, field: string, current: object) {
        // the column's options are read here, at gesture time — a Column's
        // `options` may be a constraint (a fresh handler read sees it; the
        // column record deliberately does not carry it)
        let opts = null
        for (const c of this.children) {
            if ((c as Column)._isColumn === true && this.colId(c) == field) opts = (c as Column).options
            }
        if (_selMenu == null) _selMenu = (this.parent as View).createView("Menu", ({ }))   // beside the grid, in its context's layer
        const m = this._selMenu as any
        m.minWidth = 170
        m.items = (opts ?? []).map((o) => ({ id: "" + o, label: "" + o, checked: "" + (current ?? "") == "" + o }))
        m.picked = (id) => { (row as GridRow).setText(field, "" + id) }
        m.openFor(cell)
        },

    // ── the header: chrome above the scroll region. The grid's box is the
    //    rows' scroll region exactly — the scrollbar spans rows only, no
    //    row ever slides under the header. A scroller clips at its box, so
    //    the header paints in the grid's parent (paintsIn — planes.md §3.1)
    //    and positions in the parent's frame; it stays the grid's member, so
    //    the column model sees nothing move, and the hit walk finds it among
    //    the parent's paint guests. ignoreLayout keeps full-mode stacking and
    //    the kernel's leading anchor off it — rows start at content 0 ────────

    hdr: View [ height = { classroot.headerH },
        paintsIn = { classroot.parent },
        x = { classroot.x },
        y = { classroot.y - classroot.headerH },

        // painting elsewhere, it leaves the grid's display subtree, so the
        // grid's own visibility does not reach it through the platform — mirror it

        visible = { classroot.visible },
        width = { classroot.width },
        ignoreLayout = true,
        ignoreClip = true,
        fill = { provided("theme").surface },
        datapath = { classroot.colData.value },
        accessibilityRole = row,

        View [ datapath = :cols[], claim = x,
            colid: string = { "" + :id },

            // a column header: named by its title, saying how its column is sorted;
            // the keyboard's position when the grid's is in the header row

            accessibilityRole = columnHeader,
            accessibilitySort = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortOn != colid ? "none" : (classroot as DataGrid).sortDir == "desc" ? "descending" : "ascending" },
            _ci: number = { 0 + (:i ?? 0) },
            activeHere: boolean = { (classroot as DataGrid).focused && (classroot as DataGrid).inHeader && (classroot as DataGrid).activeCol == _ci },
            trackChanges = ["activeHere"],
            onChange(e: ChangeEvent) {
                const g = classroot as DataGrid
                if (activeHere) g.activeView = this
                else if (g.activeView == this) g.activeView = null
                },
            x = { dragging && moved ? dragX : :x },
            width = { :width },
            height = 100%,

            // dragging lifts the header: an opaque ghost above its
            // neighbors (raise + surface fill + edge), tracking the pointer;
            // the grid's insertion bar shows the would-be slot; the order
            // commits at release (drop-commit — no mid-drag re-derives)

            dragging: boolean = false,
            moved: boolean = false,
            dragX: number = 0,
            x0: number = 0,
            p0: number = 0,

            // the lifted ghost is unmistakably in hand: an accent-edged,
            // shadowed, rounded card floating over its neighbors

            fill = { dragging && moved ? provided("theme").surface : null },
            stroke = { dragging && moved ? stroke(1.5, provided("theme").accent) : activeHere ? stroke(2, provided("theme").accent) : null },
            shadow = { dragging && moved ? (provided("theme").dragShadow) : null },
            cornerRadius = { dragging && moved ? 6 : activeHere ? provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius : 0 },
            opacity = { dragging && moved ? 0.95 : 1 },

            onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
                if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
                dragging = true
                moved = false
                x0 = this.x
                dragX = this.x
                p0 = app.pointerX
                },
            onPointerMove() {
                if (!dragging) return
                const dx = app.pointerX - p0
                if (!moved && Math.abs(dx) > 3) { moved = true; this.raise() }
                dragX = x0 + dx
                if (moved) {
                    (classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol = colid
                    ;(classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt = (classroot as DataGrid)._dropIndexFor(dragX + :width / 2)
                    }
                },
            onPointerUp() {
                if (!dragging) return
                dragging = false
                if (moved) (classroot as DataGrid)._commitDrop(colid)
                // an unmoved press is the click — the sort delivery
                else (classroot as DataGrid).headerClick(colid)
                },

            lbl: TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridHeaderFontSize }, fontWeight = semibold, wrap = false,
                textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted },
                text = { "" + :title } ],

            // The sort direction, drawn: one chevron class, turned by its
            // `dir`, so a sort header agrees with every other disclosure mark
            // in the chrome.

            sortMark: ChevronIcon [ y = center, iconSize = 11,
                x = { parent.width - this.width - 14 },
                visible = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortOn == parent.colid },
                ink = { provided("theme").textMuted },
                dir = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortDir == "desc" ? "down" : "up" } ],

            // the resize grip: the left-edge sliver, resizing the previous
            // column — the boundary region belongs to this cell (later
            // siblings hit first), so the grip must live here to be
            // reachable; absent on the first column. Plain widths delivered
            // through resizeInput — nothing measures anything.

            grip: View [ width = 10, height = 100%,
                x = -5,
                visible = { (:x ?? 0) > 0 },
                cursor = "col-resize", claim = x,
                resizing: boolean = false,
                pid: string = "",
                w0: number = 0,
                g0: number = 0,
                onPointerDown(e: PointerEvent) {
                    if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
                    pid = (classroot as DataGrid)._prevColId(this.parent.colid)
                    if (pid == "") return
                    w0 = (classroot as DataGrid).colWidthOf(pid)
                    g0 = app.pointerX
                    resizing = true
                    },
                onPointerMove() {
                    if (!resizing) return
                    (classroot as DataGrid).resizeInput(pid, Math.max(40, w0 + app.pointerX - g0))
                    },
                onPointerUp() { resizing = false }
                ],

            hair: View [ width = 1, height = { parent.height - 10 }, y = 5,
                x = { parent.width - 1 }, fill = { provided("theme").line } ]
            ],

        // the insertion bar — where the dragged column would land

        drop: View [ width = 2, y = 2, height = { parent.height - 4 },
            visible = { (classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol != "" },
            x = { (classroot as DataGrid)._dropBarX((classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt) - 1 },
            fill = { provided("theme").accent } ],

        // the hairline under the header. It stays a child view rather than
        // the header's own bottom stroke: a per-side stroke is a bare list,
        // and its colour here is a theme read.

        rule: View [ x = 0, width = 100%, height = 1,
            y = { parent.height - 1 }, fill = { provided("theme").line } ]
        ]
    ]


// The row: TableRow's whole contract (selection, item, the click wiring),
// generating one cell per column record. The use site writes it bare over
// its rows path — the cells come from the grid's column model.

/*
# GridRow

The row template of a `DataGrid`: write one bare `GridRow` over the rows path
(`GridRow [ datapath = :rows[] ]`) and its cells generate from the column model, each
showing `record[field]`. Selection, keyboard travel and windowing are `TableRow`'s and
`Table`'s, unchanged; `virtualize = true` on the template windows the grid.

```declare
App [ width = 400, height = 200,
    d: Dataset [ contents = { { rows: [...Array(500).keys()].map((i) => ({ id: i, n: "row " + i })) } } ],
    DataGrid [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 300, height = 150, datapath = { d.value },
        Column [ title = "Name", field = "n", width = 280 ],
        GridRow [ datapath = :rows[], virtualize = true ]
        ]
    ]
```

## rec
The row's record, as a derived attribute. **An attribute rather than a method call, so cells
re-derive when the record's fields change** — a method read would go stale. `null` on a
written (unreplicated) row.

## setText()
Writes a text value into the field a column names, in **this row's** record —
`:@[(field)] = v`. A cell calls it rather than writing itself, because a cell's own
cursor is its column, not the row.

## setFlag()
The same for a boolean field — what a `"check"` cell calls.

## trackChanges
Empty: in a grid the keyboard is on a cell, and the cell reports itself, not the row.

## _isGridRow
**Internal.** The marker identifying a generated grid row — not API.

## height
From the theme's `gridRowHeight` (28 by default); the header row is `gridHeaderHeight` (30).
*/

class GridRow extends TableRow [
    accessibilityRole = row,
    _isGridRow: boolean = true,
    trackChanges = [],               // in a grid the keyboard is on a cell, which reports itself

    height = { provided("theme").gridRowHeight },

    // the row's record as a derived attribute — the read is analyzed, so cells
    // re-render when the record's fields change (a method call would go
    // stale); null when written/unreplicated

    rec: object = { this.datapath != null ? :@ : null },

    // a cell's edit lands in this row's record, in the field its column names —
    // written here, on the row, because a cell's own cursor is its column

    setText(field: string, v: string)  { :@[(field)] = v },
    setFlag(field: string, v: boolean) { :@[(field)] = v },

    cells: View [ x = 0, y = 0,
        width = 100%,
        height = 100%,
        datapath = { (classroot.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true ? (classroot.parent as DataGrid).colData.value : null },

        // One cell per column record, built as the class its column's `kind`
        // names — each kind builds only its own presentation. The row wires
        // itself into each cell; editors write the record through it
        // (setText / setFlag), so the grid never holds cell state, and
        // windowing and sort re-derives keep every edit.

        GridCell [ datapath = :cols[],
            classFor = { ({ edit: EditCell, check: CheckCell, select: SelectCell })[:kind] ?? TextCell },
            row = { classroot } ]
        ]
    ]


/*
# GridCell

The base of a `DataGrid` cell: one per column in each row, placed and sized from the column
model, its datapath the column's record. The row sets `row`, so a cell reads the row's record
and writes through it. The grid builds each cell as the class its column's `kind` names:
`TextCell`, `EditCell`, `CheckCell` or `SelectCell`.

## row
The `GridRow` this cell belongs to, set by the row. A cell reads `row.rec` and writes with
`row.setText` / `row.setFlag`, because its own cursor is its column, not the row.

## value
The record's value in this cell's field, read through the row — `null` when the row has no
record.

## _k
**Internal.** The column's `kind`, as a string.

## _fld
**Internal.** The field this cell shows — its column's `field`.

## _ci
**Internal.** Its column's place in the column model — what the grid's `activeCol` names.

## dataGrid
The `DataGrid` its row belongs to, or null under a plain table.

## activeHere
Whether it is the grid's active cell while the grid has focus.

## trackChanges
`activeHere`, so the cell can tell its grid when it becomes the active one.

## onChange
The cell that becomes the grid's active cell records itself as the grid's `activeView`,
which the grid names to a screen reader; one that stops being it takes itself back.

## x
Its column's offset in the row.

## width
Its column's width.

## stroke
The outline that marks the active cell.

## cornerRadius
From the theme's `gridCellCornerRadius` (3 by default): the active cell's outline. A select cell's button sits 2 rounder.
*/

class GridCell [ x = { :x }, width = { :width }, height = 100%, clip = true,
    accessibilityRole = cell,
    row: GridRow? = null,
    _k: string = { "" + (:kind ?? "text") },
    _fld: string = { "" + :field },
    _ci: number = { 0 + (:i ?? 0) },
    value: object = { this.row != null && this.row.rec != null ? (this.row.rec as any)["" + :field] : null },

    // the active cell — the grid's keyboard position — is outlined while the
    // grid has focus

    dataGrid: DataGrid? = { this.row != null && (this.row.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true ? this.row.parent as DataGrid : null },
    gridFocused: boolean = { this.dataGrid != null && this.dataGrid.focused && !this.dataGrid.inHeader },
    activeHere: boolean = { this.gridFocused && this.row?.active == true && this.dataGrid?.activeCol == this._ci },
    stroke = { this.activeHere ? stroke(2, provided("theme").accent) : null },
    cornerRadius = { provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius },

    // the active cell says so to its grid, which names it to a screen reader

    trackChanges = ["activeHere"],
    onChange(e: ChangeEvent) {
        const g = this.dataGrid
        if (g == null) return
        if (this.activeHere) g.activeView = this
        else if (g.activeView == this) g.activeView = null
        },

    // a cell's control taking focus — by a press or by Tab inside the row —
    // makes this the active cell

    takeActive() {
        const g = this.dataGrid
        const r = this.row
        if (g == null || r == null) return
        g.activeCol = this._ci
        const i = r.tableIndex()
        g.activeIndex = i
        g.active = g.itemAt(i)
        }
    ]


/*
# TextCell

A `DataGrid` cell that shows its value as a label — the default `kind`, `"text"`.
*/

class TextCell extends GridCell [
    t: TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, wrap = false, text = { "" + (classroot.value ?? "") } ]
    ]


/*
# EditCell

A `DataGrid` cell that edits its value in a `TextInput`, committing every keystroke into the
record — `kind = "edit"`. Up and Down move the edit to the same column in the row above or
below (`DataGrid.editStep`).

## _before
**Internal.** The value when the edit began, which Esc puts back.
*/

class EditCell extends GridCell [
    _before: string = "",      // the value when the edit began, for Esc to restore

    ed: TextInput [ x = 4, y = 3, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize },
        accessibilityLabel = { "" + (:title ?? "") },                // named by its column
        width = { parent.width - 8 },
        height = { parent.height - 6 },
        initial = { "" + (classroot.value ?? "") },
        onFocus() { classroot._before = "" + (classroot.value ?? ""); classroot.takeActive() },
        onInput(v: string) { if (classroot.row != null) classroot.row.setText(classroot._fld, v) },
        onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
            const r = classroot.row
            if (r == null || (r.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
            if (e.key == "ArrowDown" || e.key == "Enter") (r.parent as DataGrid).editStep(r, classroot._fld, 1)
            else if (e.key == "ArrowUp") (r.parent as DataGrid).editStep(r, classroot._fld, -1)
            else if (e.key == "Escape") r.setText(classroot._fld, classroot._before)
            }
        ]
    ]


/*
# CheckCell

A `DataGrid` cell that shows a boolean value as a `Checkbox` and writes it back —
`kind = "check"`.
*/

class CheckCell extends GridCell [
    ck: Checkbox [ label = "", x = center, y = center,
        accessibilityLabel = { "" + (:title ?? "") },                // named by its column
        onFocus() { super.onFocus(); classroot.takeActive() },
        checked = { classroot.value == true },
        input(v: boolean) { if (classroot.row != null) classroot.row.setFlag(classroot._fld, v) }
        ]
    ]


/*
# SelectCell

A `DataGrid` cell that shows its value with a chevron and, pressed, opens the grid's shared
options menu (`DataGrid.openSelect`) — `kind = "select"`. Its button is a `Control`: Space or
Enter opens the menu.
*/

class SelectCell extends GridCell [
    // a Control — a real tab stop (Space/Enter opens the menu, the Control
    // activation path), styled as a quiet button so it reads as interactive

    sel: Control [ x = 2, y = 2,
        width = { parent.width - 4 },
        height = { parent.height - 4 },
        cornerRadius = { (provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius) + 2 },
        fill = { this.pressed ? provided("theme").controlPressed : this.hovered ? provided("theme").controlHovered : null },
        onFocus() { super.onFocus(); classroot.takeActive() },
        press() {
            const r = classroot.row
            if (r == null || (r.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
            (r.parent as DataGrid).openSelect(r, this, classroot._fld, classroot.value)
            },
        onClick() { if (!this.disabled) this.press() },
        v: TextLabel [ x = 6, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, wrap = false, text = { "" + (classroot.value ?? "") } ],
        arrow: ChevronIcon [ y = center, iconSize = 11,
            x = { parent.width - this.width - 6 },
            ink = { provided("theme").textMuted } ]
        ]
    ]
